              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Training Examples**\n\nI'm currently diving into the training examples. Specifically, I am examining the first one, which involves a 4x9 grid divided by a color. The split occurs at column 4, and I'm noting the color distribution on each resulting side, particularly colors 1, 3, and 4. I'm noting the distribution of these colors on the left and right sides.\n\n\n**Deciphering the Pattern**\n\nI've got it! I've been dissecting the relationship between the input grid and the output. It looks like I was right! The core rule revolves around the most common non-background color on each side. If the dominant non-background color is the same on both sides, the output is 0. If they are different, the output is determined by this pattern. Let's see if this rule consistently holds as I analyze more examples.\n\n\n**Confirming The Hypothesis**\n\nI'm now running more tests. Based on this deeper dive into several examples, I am validating a preliminary hypothesis. My current thought is that it is symmetric difference. The most recent data aligns with this, showing that when one side is the background and the other a different color, the non-background color prevails! Also, when there are conflicts, the output is black/background. Let's look at Example 2.\n\n\n",
