              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Training Examples**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the first training example. I've identified the input format and how the data is split into left and right components. Color frequencies, particularly the background color (assumed to be 1), are being noted. The goal is to establish the encoding principles from these raw data points.\n\n\n**Confirming XOR Logic**\n\nI've just confirmed the XOR logic hypothesis. Analyzing the first row, if only the right component has the foreground color, the output uses that color. Conversely, if only the left has it, the output mirrors the left component's color. If both have the foreground or neither, the output becomes 0 (background). This appears consistent across the first row, so let's check the subsequent rows.\n\n\n**Validating Pattern Consistency**\n\nThe XOR pattern holds true! I've exhaustively tested additional rows and examples. I verified the color selection based on the presence/absence of foreground colors in left and right components, observing that the output consistently adheres to the XOR rule. Even with different dimensions (5x5 instead of 4x4) and varying foreground/background color combinations, the rule holds.\n\n\n**Extending XOR Analysis**\n\nI'm now extending the XOR analysis to a 9x5 grid. I'm verifying the XOR rule's consistent application across varied inputs. With the split and foreground colors defined as expected, the rule still holds for each row. The output, as defined by XOR logic, perfectly aligns with expectations. It's a 9x5 grid instead of a 4x4.\n\n\n",
